From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 18:02:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: cdns3: sky1: keep the wrapper pinned by its children

Patch 0180 added pm_suspend_ignore_children(dev, true) to silence two cosmetic
runtime-PM warnings.  It also decouples the CIXH2030 wrapper from its
children's PM accounting, so the wrapper can runtime-suspend while its xHCI
child is active -- and a suspended wrapper does not service port-change
events.

Measured on the O6N installer, stick in a USB-A port:

  portsc = 0x0c0006e1  Speed=1 Link=Polling CCS PP WDE WOE

CCS=1 (a device IS connected) and PP=1 (port powered), but PED=0 and
PLS=Polling -- the port was never reset or enabled, and dmesg carries zero hub
port errors because the hub driver was never told to look.  Unbinding and
rebinding the hub driver on the root-hub interfaces enumerates the whole tree
immediately and /proc/partitions gains sda + its partitions.

Runtime-PM state, 7.2 vs the working 7.0.12:

  7.0.12 MS-R1  (works)   CIXH2030 wrappers active,    children active
  7.2    O6N    (broken)  CIXH2030 wrappers SUSPENDED, children active

That is the only behavioural divergence in the whole USB glue: cdnsp-plat.c is
byte-identical between the two trees and cdnsp-sky1.c differs otherwise only
in two static additions and an -ENOENT -> -EPROBE_DEFER clock change.

Drop the call and let the always-active children pin the parent, as 7.0.12
does.  The cost is the return of the cosmetic warning 0180 silenced.

The rest of 0180 -- ordering the wrapper's activation before child population
-- is correct and is kept.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/cdns3/cdnsp-sky1.c
@@ -768,14 +768,23 @@
 	 * the children are enumerated asynchronously, so activating the parent
 	 * first makes the child activation legal in both cases.
 	 *
-	 * pm_suspend_ignore_children() additionally decouples this always-on
-	 * register/clock glue -- whose runtime suspend/resume are no-ops --
-	 * from the children's runtime-PM accounting, which also closes the
-	 * asynchronous ACPI probe-order window.
+	 * Do NOT call pm_suspend_ignore_children() here.  Decoupling the
+	 * wrapper from its children's runtime-PM accounting lets the wrapper
+	 * runtime-suspend while its xHCI child is active, and a suspended
+	 * wrapper does not service port-change events: the port sits at
+	 * PLS=Polling with CCS=1 (device present, never reset), the hub driver
+	 * is never told, and nothing enumerates.  Measured on O6N 7.2 vs the
+	 * working 7.0.12 on MS-R1:
+	 *
+	 *   7.0.12 MS-R1 (works)  CIXH2030 wrappers active,    children active
+	 *   7.2    O6N   (broken) CIXH2030 wrappers SUSPENDED, children active
+	 *
+	 * Letting the always-active children pin the parent -- which is what
+	 * 7.0.12 does -- costs only the cosmetic "Enabling runtime PM for
+	 * inactive device with active children" warning this had silenced.
 	 */
 	device_set_wakeup_capable(dev, true);
 	pm_runtime_set_active(dev);
-	pm_suspend_ignore_children(dev, true);
 	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
 
 	if (!ACPI_COMPANION(dev)) {
